{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>I have heard the complaints of the children of Israel. Speak to them, saying, Between the evenings</b> — This statement Moses had already spoken to Israel (Exodus 16:8), but it was repeated because it says, <b>I have heard the complaints of the children of Israel</b>. For at first He said (Exodus 16:4), <b>Behold, I will rain bread for you</b>, as one doing kindness with them of His own will or for their merit, but now He declared that it is reckoned for them as a sin, and because of their complaint He will do so with them in order that they may know through it that I am YHWH your Elohim, for until now you do not believe in YHWH your Elohim; therefore you complain against His prophets. And it is possible that at first He did not promise them that the manna would be theirs all the days of the wilderness, and they thought perhaps it would be for one day or two while they were in that place, and when they journeyed from there they would come to a place of bread. But now He said to them, <b>Between the evenings you shall eat meat</b> regularly, and every morning you shall be sated with bread all the days of the wilderness. And so is the opinion of our Sages, that the quail was with them from that day onward like the manna, and so it appears. For they complained about two things, and in both He heard their complaint and would fulfill their craving — for what would He give them, and what would He add to them meat for one day or two? And the passage elaborates at length concerning the matter of the manna, for all His deeds are wondrous, but is brief concerning the matter of the quail, <b>And it came to pass at evening, and the quail came up</b> (verse 13), for it is in the usual way of the world. And the matter of the quail a second time at Kivrot HaTa'avah (Numbers 11:31) — that did not come to them now for satiety, as He says here, regularly (in verses 8 and 12), <b>meat to eat and bread to be sated</b>. And it is possible that their elders would gather it, or that it would present itself to the pious among them, while their younger ones craved it and went hungry for it, for concerning the quail it does not say, <b>And they gathered, the much and the little</b>, as He says concerning the manna. And therefore it says there (Numbers 11:4), 'And the rabble that was among them craved a craving,' and it says (ibid.), 'And the children of Israel also wept again' — that some of them too wept for it, but not all of them. And then He gave them from it in great abundance, as He says (ibid. v. 32), 'He who gathered least gathered ten homers,' and they ate a month of days from that abundance, and it returned to its former manner. And according to the plain sense, all the dealings of the quail were occasional, while the manna, which was their life, was theirs continually, for their principal complaint was about it, as it is written, <b>For you have brought us out into this wilderness to kill this whole assembly with hunger</b>."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:17:08.757242415Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}